  "image_name": "0a074e4e7a59bd1d45b46637691004a3.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the cell cycle and division pattern of a flagellated protozoan cell, showing how a new flagellum and associated structures are built before the cell splits into two daughters. Across the main circular sequence, an original cell with one nucleus, one basal body, one flagellum, and a flagellum attachment zone (FAZ) produces a new basal body and a short new flagellum. As this new flagellum elongates alongside the old one, a new FAZ also forms. A small connector appears to keep the growing new flagellum associated with the old flagellum. The cleavage furrow then begins near the anterior region between the two flagella/FAZ paths and progresses to separate the cell longitudinally. The result is two daughter cells, each inheriting a nucleus and a flagellum system, with the old and new flagella ending up in different daughters.\n\nThe lower schematic appears to compare how this can generate daughters of unequal morphology during development: one daughter retains the long, attached flagellum, while the other may become a shorter cell with a remodeled or shorter flagellum. Overall, the image emphasizes coordinated duplication of cytoskeletal landmarks and polarized cytokinesis.",
